  "result": "Maintenance of Cultures (https://sagdb.uni-goettingen.de/culture_media/28%20WEES%20Medium.pdf)\nciteturn21view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 3; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://sagdb.uni-goettingen.de/culture_media/28%20WEES%20Medium.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 114\nL0@P0: Sammlung von Algenkulturen Göttingen Medium Recipe\nL1@P0: Culture Collection of Algae Vers. 10.2008\nL2@P0: Maintenance of Cultures\nL3@P0: Cultures sent to customers remain alive for at least 14 days provided that the following\nL4@P0: precautions are observed. Cultures should be unpacked immediately after receipt and stored at\nL5@P0: 15-18°C under low light intensity (north window, no direct sun light, or weak white\nL6@P0: fluorescent light). Screw caps or vessels should be loosened but not removed. Further\nL7@P0: maintenance or multiplication of cultures requires transfer into new culture media. This\nL8@P0: presupposes experience in simple microbial techniques.\nL9@P0: Many species are cultivated and dispatched on agar media for safety reasons but develop their\nL10@P0: morphological characteristics only in liquid media, e.g. flagellates, colony-forming\nL11@P0: Volvocales and Chlorococcales. For teaching purposes these species should be transferred\nL12@P0: into liquid media 2-3 weeks before demonstration, e.g. into Soil Water Media, Basal Medium,\nL13@P0: or Desmidiacean Medium.\nL14@P0: Culture Media\nL15@P0: The following media have proved suitable for the maintenance of cultures in test tubes at the\nL16@P0: SAG for many years. The recipes originate from E. G. Pringsheim and W. Koch, unless stated\nL17@P0: otherwise. It must be emphasized that the maintenance medium indicated is not always the\nL18@P0: best medium for the cultivation of a species. There are other media which are just as suitable,\nL19@P0: e.g. those given in the catalogues of other culture collections of algae (Thompson et al., 1988,\nL20@P0: Watanabe and Nozaki, 1994, Andersen et al., 1991, Rippka and Herdman, 1992, Starr and\nL21@P0: Zeikus, 1993). Mass algal culture often requires more concentrated media (for recipes and\nL22@P0: methods consult Kuhl and Lorenzen, 1964; Starr, 1971; Stein, 1973; Guillard, 1975; Werner,\nL23@P0: 1982; Castenholz, 1988; Richmond, 2004; Andersen, 2005).\nL24@P0: All solutions should be made up with de-ionized water. Media are usually prepared from\nL25@P0: stock solutions of macronutrients, trace metals, and vitamins which are added to a large\nL26@P0: proportion of the final volume of water in order to avoid precipitation.\nL27@P0: Media may be used as liquid or solidified by 1.0-1.5% agar. Before sterilization the agar has\nL28@P0: to be dissolved in the medium in a steamer. After this test tubes should be filled with 10 ml of\nL29@P0: the hot medium, closed with cotton plugs, sterilized (usually by autoclaving at 121°C for 15\nL30@P0: min.) and may be stored for several weeks, after cooling, in a refrigerator. Solid media for\nL31@P0: Cyanobacteria are prepared by mixing, after cooling to 50°C, equal volumes of separately\nL32@P0: autoclaved double strength solutions of the mineral salts medium and either agar to give a\nL33@P0: final agar concentration of 0.6-1.0 %.\nL34@P0-1: www. epsag.uni-goettingen.de Sammlung von Algenkulturen Göttingen Medium Recipe\nL35@P1: Culture Collection of Algae Version 03.2007\nL36@P1: 28. WEES Medium\nL37@P1: mL/ L de-ionized or distilled H2O stock solution concentration\nL38@P1: 1 KNO3 10 %\nL39@P1: 1 MgSO4\nL40@P1: .\nL41@P1:  7H2O 2 %\nL42@P1: 1 (NH4)2HPO4 2 %\nL43@P1: 1 CaSO4 saturated\nL44@P1: 1 P II metal mix without Fe\nL45@P1: 1 Fe-EDTA\nL46@P1: 1 vitamin solution\nL47@P1: 100 soil extract\nL48@P1: Autoclave at 121°C for 30 min. Adjust pH to 5,4-6,0 with 0,1 n HCl.\nL49@P1: Preparation of PII metal mix without Fe:\nL50@P1:  in 100 ml de-ionized or distilled water\nL51@P1: EDTA 0.3 g\nL52@P1: H3BO3 0.114 g\nL53@P1: MnCl2 . 4 H2O 14.4 mg\nL54@P1: ZnSO4 . 7 H2O 2.1 mg\nL55@P1: CoCl2 . 6 H2O 0.4 mg\nL56@P1: Preparation of Fe-EDTA:\nL57@P1:  in 100 ml de-ionized or distilled water\nL58@P1: EDTA (not as Na-salt) 0.52 g\nL59@P1: FeSO4 . 7 H2O 0.5 g\nL60@P1: 1 n KOH 5.4 ml\nL61@P1: \nL62@P1: Preparation of vitamin stock solution:\nL63@P1:  in 1000 ml de-ionized or distilled water\nL64@P1: vitamin B12 0.2 mg\nL65@P1: Biotine 1.0 mg\nL66@P1: Thiamine-HCl 100 mg\nL67@P1: Nicotinamide 0.1 mg\nL68@P1: Preparation of soil extract:\nL69@P1: Place 10 g of garden soil in a bottle, add 125 ml de-ionized or distilled water and cover with\nL70@P1: cotton plug. Boil for 5 min, filtrate and centrifugate extract, add de-ionized or distilled water\nL71@P1: up to 100 ml.\nL72@P1: \nL73@P1: Ref.: Kies, L. – Oogamie bei Eremosphaera viridis De Bary. Flora, Abt. B 157 (1967), 1-12.\nL74@P1-2: www.epsag.uni-goettingen.de Sammlung von Algenkulturen Göttingen Medium Recipe\nL75@P2: Culture Collection of Algae Vers. 10.2008\nL76@P2: References\nL77@P2: Andersen, R.A.; Jacobson, D.M. & Sexton, J.P. – Provasoli-Guillard Center for Culture of Marine\nL78@P2: Phytoplankton. Catalogue of Strains. 98pp. West Boothbay Harbor, Maine, USA, 1991.\nL79@P2: Castenholz, R.W. – Culturing methods for Cyanobacteria. In: L. Packer and A.N. Glazer, eds., Cyanobacteria.\nL80@P2: Methods of Enzymology 167 (1988), 68-93.\nL81@P2: Guillard, R.R.L. – Culture of Phytoplankton for feeding marine invertebrates. In: W.L. Smith and M. H.\nL82@P2: Chanley, eds., Culture of marine invertebrate animals. pp.29-60, Plenum Book Publ. Corp., New York,\nL83@P2: 1975.\nL84@P2: Kuhl, A. & Lorenzen, H. – Handling and culturing of Chlorella. In: D.M. Prescott, ed., Methods in cell\nL85@P2: physiology. Vol.1, pp. 152-187, Academic Press, New York and London, 1964.\nL86@P2: Rippka, R. & Herdman, M. – Pasteur Culture Collection of Cyanobacterial Strains in Axenic Culture. Vol.1,\nL87@P2: Catalogue of strains. 103pp., Institut Pasteur, Paris, France, 1992.\nL88@P2: Starr, R.C. – Algal Cultures – sources and methods of cultivation. In: A. San Pietro, ed., Photosynthesis. Part A,\nL89@P2: pp. 29-53, Methods in Enzymology vol. 23, Academic Press, New York, 1971.\nL90@P2: Starr, R.C. & Zeikus, J.A. – UTEX – The Culture Collection of Algae at the University of Texas at Austin. J.\nL91@P2: Phycol. Suppl. 29 (1993).\nL92@P2: Stein, J.R. ed. – Handbook of phycological methods. Culture Methods and growth measurements, pp. 448,\nL93@P2: Cambridge at the University Press, London, New York, 1973.\nL94@P2: Thompson, A.S.; Rhodes, J.C. & Pettman, I. – Culture Collection of Algae and Protozoa. Catalogue of strains.\nL95@P2: 164pp., Natural Environment Research and Council, England, 5th edit., 1988.\nL96@P2: Watanabe, M.M. & Nozaki, H. – NIES-Collection. List of strains, microalgae and protozoa. 4th edit., 127pp. The\nL97@P2: National Institute for Environmental Studies, Japan, 1994.\nL98@P2: Werner, D. – Biologische Versuchobjekte. Kultivierung und Wachstum ausgewählter Versuchsorganismen in\nL99@P2: definierten Medien. 432pp. Fischer Verlag, Stuttgart, New York, 1982.\nL100@P2: Further recommended literature about culturing algae:\nL101@P2: Andersen, R.A. (ed.) (2005) Algal Culturing Techniques. Elsevier Academic Press, Burlington.\nL102@P2:  ISBN 0-12-088426-7.\nL103@P2: Belcher & Swale (1982) Culturing Algae - a guide for schools and colleges.\nL104@P2: ISBN 1-871105-04-8 (ask for at ccap@sams.ac.uk). (Currently unavailable).\nL105@P2: Isaac & Jennings (1995) Microbial Culture. Bios Scientific Publ., Oxford.\nL106@P2: ISBN 1-872748-92-9.\nL107@P2: Richmond (ed.) (2004) Handbook of Microalgal Culture. Biotechnology\nL108@P2: and Applied Phycology. Blackwell Publ., London. ISBN 0-632-05953-2.\nL109@P2: Streble & Krauter (2006) Das Leben im Wassertropfen. Kosmos (Franckh-Kosmos), Stuttgart.\nL110@P2: ISBN 3-440-10807-4.\nL111@P2: Von Berg, Linne & Melkonian (2004) Der Kosmos-Algenführer. Kosmos (Franckh-Kosmos), Stuttgart.\nL112@P2: ISBN 3-440-09719-6.\nL113@P2: www. epsag.uni-goettingen.de\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMedium data (https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=629)\nciteturn21view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=629\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 26\nL0: # Medium data\nL1: \nL2: Search for medium no. = [629].\nL3: \nL4: * * *\nL5: \nL6: Unless otherwise stated, sterilize media by autoclaving at 121°C for 15 min.\nL7: \nL8: * * *\nL9: \nL10: 629   SALTWATER IRON-OXIDIZING BACTERIA MEDIUM\nL11: Use Medium No. 【0†628】 with artificial saltwater (see below) instead of Modified Wolfe's mineral solution.\nL12: Artificial saltwater:\nL13: NaCl  | ` 27.5    `  | g\nL14: MgCl_{2}·6H_{2}O  | ` 5.38   `  | g\nL15: MgSO_{4}·7H_{2}O  | ` 6.78   `  | g\nL16: KCl  | ` 0.72   `  | g\nL17: NaHCO_{3}  | ` 0.2    `  | g\nL18: CaCl_{2}·2H_{2}O  | ` 1.4    `  | g\nL19: NH_{4}Cl  | ` 1.0    `  | g\nL20: KH_{2}PO_{4}  | ` 0.05   `  | g\nL21: Distilled water  | ` 1.0    `  | L\nL22: \nL23: * * *\nL24: \nL25: Copyright © 2026 Microbe Division (JCM) - All Rights Reserved\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn21view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=1118\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=1118 is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.dsmz.de/microorganisms/medium/pdf/DSMZ_Medium668.pdf)\nciteturn21view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 1; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.dsmz.de/microorganisms/medium/pdf/DSMZ_Medium668.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 20\nL0@P0: © 2007 DSMZ GmbH - All rights reserved\nL1@P0: 668. FLEXIBACTER MEDIUM\nL2@P0: Tryptone (Difco 0123) 1.00 g\nL3@P0: Vitamin-free casamino acids 1.00 g\nL4@P0: Na-glutamate 5.00 g\nL5@P0: Na-glycerophosphate 0.10 g\nL6@P0: Vitamin B12 1.00 μg\nL7@P0: Ho-le trace element solution (see below) 1.00 ml\nL8@P0: Agar (if needed) 15.00 g\nL9@P0: Filtered sea water to 1000.00 ml\nL10@P0: Trace element solution Ho-le:\nL11@P0: H3BO3 2.85 g\nL12@P0: MnCl2 x 4 H2O 1.80 g\nL13@P0: FeSO4 1.36 g\nL14@P0: Na-tartrate 1.77 g\nL15@P0: CuCl2 x 2 H2O 26.90 mg\nL16@P0: ZnCl2 20.80 mg\nL17@P0: CoCl2 x 6 H2O 40.40 mg\nL18@P0: Na2MoO4 x 2 H2O 25.20 mg\nL19@P0: Distilled water 1000.00 ml\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMedium data (https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=426)\nciteturn21view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.jcm.riken.jp/cgi-bin/jcm/jcm_grmd?GRMD=426\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 17\nL0: # Medium data\nL1: \nL2: Search for medium no. = [426].\nL3: \nL4: * * *\nL5: \nL6: Unless otherwise stated, sterilize media by autoclaving at 121°C for 15 min.\nL7: \nL8: * * *\nL9: \nL10: 426   BEER MEDIUM\nL11: \nL12: Prepare MRS medium (see Medium No. 【0†1】) in normally hopped beer instead of distilled water. Adjust pH to 5.2.\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copyright © 2026 Microbe Division (JCM) - All Rights Reserved\n"
